        --- FAIL: TestUpdateSettings_SaveError (0.00s)
        --- FAIL: TestCRUD_SaveErrors (0.01s)
        FAIL	budget2/internal/services/retirement	0.014s
    Five tests killed it before, none kill it now.

    Root cause: corruptSettingsDirToFile deletes the settings directory's
    contents and replaces the directory with a plain file, so
    loadInternal's own store.MkdirAll(settingsDir) fails ENOTDIR FIRST.
    Every one of these tests now returns an error from the LOAD, never
    reaching the save it is named for. The old chmod 0500 kept the directory
    readable and its file readable, so the load succeeded and only the write
    failed -- which is what made the tests save-path tests. This is exactly
    the trap the worker documented in makeSaveFail's own comment ("the test
    would be proving 'load breaks' not 'save fails,' and a mutation to the
    save path alone would go undetected") and then did not avoid in
    corruptSettingsDirToFile.

    The same regression hits whatif's makeSaveFail (identical body). Against
    mutation (b):
      REWORKED whatif 30, as root:  11 --- FAIL
      REWORKED whatif 30, as nobody: 10 --- FAIL
      ORIGINAL (HEAD) whatif 30 test files in the same mutated tree, as
      nobody: 27 --- FAIL (every _SaveError except SwitchScenario_Generic-
      Error, DeleteScenario_NonTypedError, RenameScenario_NonTypedError).
    So ~16 whatif tests plus 5 retirement tests -- about 21 of the 56 --
    have lost the save-path mutation detection they had at uid != 0.
    Four of the five required kills reproduce; (b) does not. Criterion 5,
    and NEXT.md's "each reworked test still kills its original mutation at
    both uids", are not met.

====================================================================
CRITERION 6 -- the config test rename is licensed by the production code. PASS
====================================================================
Read internal/config/config.go:151-166. The whole non-IsNotExist branch is:
    data, err := os.ReadFile(c.UserSettingsFile)
    if err != nil {
        if os.IsNotExist(err) { return make(map[string]interface{}), nil }
        return nil, err
    }
One `return nil, err` for every read error; nothing keys on the error kind.
Command: `grep -rn "IsPermission\|ErrPermission\|EACCES\|EPERM\|fs.ErrPermission"
internal/config/` -> no matches.
Command: `grep -n "IsNotExist\|errors.Is\|errors.As" internal/config/config.go`
-> a single line, 154: `if os.IsNotExist(err) {`.
The production code does not distinguish permission errors anywhere in the
package, so PermissionError -> ReadError is an honest rename, and the EISDIR
injection lands in the identical branch. Mutant (d) above confirms the
renamed test still defends that branch.

